  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1125 AM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nA ridge of high pressure aloft over the south-central CONUS,\ncentered over the western Gulf Coast, will maintain dry weather\nand above-average temps for most through the end of the work week.\nSome low rain/storm chances are introduced on Friday mainly to the\nnorth and west of Tulsa as an upper disturbance lifts out of NM\nand into KS and its plume of deep layer moisture spreads into the\nPlains. This system will top/suppress the ridge this weekend and\nslide east/northeast. The latest CAM and global model data are not\naggressive with rain coverage over our area. The model blend (NBM)\ngave our area chance PoPs with the update this morning (with some\nlow end likelies near the KS border) and this seems okay for now.\nIt does not look like a wash-out however, for those making outdoor\nplans.\n\nMulti-model ensemble means continue to point toward a more \nwidespread precipitation event next week. Timing in current data \nis next Tuesday into Wednesday. In what is definitely a sign of \nthe changing seasons, an upper trough and associated cold front is\nexpected to sweep across the country during this time.\n\nLacy",
